a_{0}+29a_{1}+37a_{2},\\qquad B=b_{0}+29b_{1}+37b_{2}.$$  |\nL336: \nL340: $D=1$. The reduction-length bound depends on the range of stored values the encoding uses: capping every stored value at $\\pm 6$ still attains coverage 148, and gives $c_{A}=c_{B}=6$ and $K^{*}=\\lfloor 2^{24}/6^{2}\\rfloor=466{,}033$, whereas an encoder that uses all of $S$ gives $c_{A}=c_{B}=12$ and $K^{*}=\\lfloor 2^{24}/12^{2}\\rfloor=116{,}508$.\nL341: An alternative 6-product solution has $w=v=(1,11,39)$ and coverage 134; its reconstruction coefficients, in the order $(P_{00},P_{11},P_{22},P_{01},P_{02},P_{12})$, are $(-49,-319,1053,11,39,429)$.\nL342: INT4$\\times$INT8 in 4 products.\nL343: \nL344:  | $$A=3a_{0}+5a_{1},\\qquad B=5b_{0}+3b_{1}+42b_{2}.$$  |\nL345: \nL346: The values of the $k$-th planes are, in order,\nL347: \nL348:  | $$(\\alpha_{k}\\cdot a)_{k=1}^{4}=(a_{1},\\ a_{0},\\ a_{0}+2a_{1},\\ 2a_{0}+a_{1}),$$  |\nL349:  | $$(\\beta_{k}\\cdot b)_{k=1}^{4}=(b_{0},\\ b_{1}-b_{2},\\ b_{2},\\ b_{0}+2b_{1}),$$  |\nL350: \nL351: and with $P_{k}=(\\alpha_{k}\\cdot a)(\\beta_{k}\\cdot b)$\nL352: \nL353:  | $$2AB=35P_{1}-42P_{2}+210P_{3}+15P_{4}$$  |\nL354: ($D=2$). An encoding that keeps the plane values within $\\pm 3$ on the $A$ side and $\\pm 8$ on the $B$ side has $c_{A}=3$, $c_{B}=8$ and $K^{*}=699{,}050$.\nL355: \nL356: Fig. 6: The range of $L$ base-13 limbs: the contiguous coverage $\\pm X_{\\mathrm{carry}}(L)$, the maximum $\\pm(13^{L}{-}1)$, and the headroom above the represented width [bit].\nL357: \nL358: FP16$\\times$FP16 in 12 products. The weights $(1,71,9,639)$ of the diagonal entry $M=12$, sorted in increasing order with the limbs renumbered:\nL359:  | $$A=a_{0}+9a_{1}+71a_{2}+639a_{3},\\qquad B=b_{0}+9b_{1}+71b_{2}+639b_{3}.$$  |\nL360: \nL361: With $P_{ij}=a_{i}b_{j}$ and $Q_{kl}=(a_{2k}+2a_{2k+1})(b_{2l}+2b_{2l+1})$ for $k,l\\in\\{0,1\\}$,\nL362: \nL363:  | $$2AB=\\sum_{k,l=0}^{1}71^{k+l}\\left(-7P_{2k,2l}+126P_{2k+1,2l+1}+9Q_{kl}\\right),$$  |\nL364: $D=2$, $c_{A}=c_{B}=12$, $K^{*}=116{,}508$. Of the weights, 9 has odd part at most 15 and fits an E4M3 block scale, whereas 71 and 639 do not and are applied in the integer reconstruction (Section cite21†IV-E ). The inner pairs $(a_{0},a_{1})$ and $(a_{2},a_{3})$, of base 9, take one Karatsuba-type step with the planes $a_{0}+2a_{1}$ and $a_{2}+2a_{3}$, and the four outer pairs, of base 71, are direct products, $4\\times 3=12$.\nL365: Figure cite68†5 shows this structure, and the form $U+sV$ of the diagonal weights describes the same construction by its outer pairs. With the plane coefficient $u=2$, extracting the cross term leaves the denominator 2 in the reconstruction coefficients, removed by the final shift of Definition cite62†IV.1 . The choice $u=2$ suits $S$ because the elements of $S$ outside the contiguous part $[-4,4]$, namely $\\pm 6,\\pm 8,\\pm 12$, are all even, and half of any even element of $S$ is again in $S$.\n"
